/* Pass25 The Shore I Choose: canonical Pass24+25 full text. */
(function(){'use strict';var lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;var edits={"maui-14":{"number":14,"title":"The Quiet Work of Rope","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — expansion pass three","content":"The rope snapped with a sound like somebody cracking a whip across the shore.\n\nI saw Maui's hand jerk backward. The mooring post shuddered. A boat that had been safely tucked against the Tideglass landing swung toward the rocks, and the two children sitting inside it screamed.\n\n\"Maui!\"\n\nHe was already moving.\n\nHe caught the bow with his hook before the boat could strike the stone. His feet planted in the wet sand, knees bent, and for a moment the whole dock seemed to strain against him. Then Fen reached the line, dragged it through the remaining ring, and tied off the boat while I helped the children onto the steps.\n\nThey were frightened. They were unhurt.\n\nThe older child tried to climb back onto the dock after Fen had tied off the boat. Their shoe slipped on wet wood and Maui made a movement to catch them, but I was already holding the rail.\n\n“Wait,” Fen said sharply. “The board beside the post is loose.”\n\nMaui froze in the middle of reaching. A single plank bowed beneath the child's foot. Tamsin appeared with a wooden brace and asked both children to sit on the dry step while she checked the fixings.\n\n“I could lift the whole dock,” Maui offered.\n\n“You could,” she said. “And then I'd have to explain to every fisher in Tideglass why the landing was gone.”\n\nMini Maui on his shoulder acted out lifting a miniature pier and being crushed by an imaginary fish. The younger child giggled. I saw Maui's expression change when he realized they were laughing, not screaming.\n\nTamsin found a snapped bolt, which she put in a cloth pouch. She asked the children what had happened before the mooring line went. The younger one said they'd heard the rope scraping; the older remembered seeing a frayed patch that morning but hadn't known whom to tell.\n\nFen crouched at the step. “You can tell any dock worker. Or me. You won't get anyone in trouble for pointing out a bad rope.”\n\nMaui rubbed his cut hand against his side.\n\n“I saw the rope,” he said after a moment. “I thought I could handle it.”\n\nTamsin looked at him. “You did handle the boat. The dock is still our job to make safe.”\n\nHe looked almost annoyed by the distinction, then nodded.\n\nOnce the children had gone home with a neighbor, I found Maui studying the bolt in the pouch.\n\n“What?” I asked.\n\n“I've fought monsters bigger than this pier.” He held up two fingers. “And a piece of iron this small nearly put two children in the water.”\n\nHe wasn't looking for comfort. He was angry at the gap between what he could lift and what he had failed to notice. I stood close enough for our shoulders to touch and let that anger be real.\n\nMaui was still smiling when I turned toward him.\n\n\"See?\" he announced, a little too loudly. \"Boat rescued. Children rescued. Hero—still devastatingly handsome.\" He gave the children a wink, then flexed his bleeding hand behind his back as though he could hide the pain from the entire shoreline.\n\nHis left hand was bleeding.\n\n\"Show me.\"\n\n\"It's a scratch.\"\n\n\"Maui.\"\n\nHe held it up. There was a raw stripe across the base of his palm, deeper at one end where the rope had caught. He flexed his fingers and tried to hide the wince under a grin.\n\n\"Hero's scratch.\"\n\n\"Well, the hero is going to sit down.\"\n\nFen looked at the broken line and swore softly.\n\n\"That shouldn't have given way.\"\n\nI checked the end. One part was frayed, but a second section looked almost polished. The strand had been rubbing against the underside of the landing, where a nail had shifted during the recent repairs.\n\n\"Maintenance failure,\" I said.\n\nFen nodded.\n\n\"Not a monster?\"\n\nMaui looked mildly offended.\n\n\"I could still fight one.\"\n\n\"Try fighting a clean bandage first.\"\n\nThe clinic didn't open until noon, but Katara had left an emergency kit in the public storage cabinet. I made Maui sit on an overturned crate while I cleaned the cut. He watched with an expression of tremendous patience that lasted perhaps thirty seconds.\n\n\"I could heal faster as a hawk.\"\n\n\"Do hawks have hands?\"\n\n\"Not technically.\"\n\n\"Then stay as you are.\"\n\nHe examined the wound over my shoulder.\n\n\"I've had worse.\"\n\n\"I believe you.\"\n\n\"Much worse.\"\n\n\"Still believe you.\"\n\n\"That time with the eel—\"\n\n\"If you tell me this cut is nothing compared to an eel, I am going to make the bandage extremely decorative.\"\n\nHe shut his mouth.\n\nFen took the broken line to Tamsin's workshop. The children went home with Mara, who offered bread and a stern lecture about staying seated while docking. By the time I finished the bandage, the tide had begun to turn.\n\nMaui reached for his hook.\n\n\"No,\" I said.\n\nHe stared at me.\n\n\"No?\"\n\n\"Not until we know whether that hand needs proper treatment. And not until somebody else examines the rest of the moorings.\"\n\n\"I can inspect them.\"\n\n\"I know you can. That's not the problem.\"\n\n\"Then what is?\"\n\nI wanted to say that he was exhausting. I wanted to say that I couldn't bear the thought of him being hurt again, because the sight of blood on his hand had made something awful open inside my chest.\n\nNeither would explain why the dock had failed.\n\n\"The problem,\" I said carefully, \"is that everyone assumes you're the solution to every emergency. Even you.\"\n\nFor a long moment he said nothing.\n\nThen he looked toward the children disappearing into the square.\n\n\"They were scared.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"And I could help.\"\n\n\"You did help. Now other people get to help too.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bandage.\n\n\"Doesn't feel right.\"\n\nThat was the most honest thing he'd said all morning.\n\nAt Tamsin's workshop, Fen showed us three knots with different jobs. One held under steady weight, one could be untied after a sudden pull, and the third was designed so a damaged line could be replaced without anyone putting a hand where the load would crush it.\n\nI got the first one wrong twice.\n\nMaui got it right immediately, then tried to demonstrate with his injured hand.\n\nMini Maui appeared at his wrist and performed an entire play about a heroic injured hand. It ended with the miniature collapsing dramatically beside a rope.\n\n\"I know,\" Maui said. \"I'm suffering.\"\n\n\"You're showing off,\" I said.\n\n\"Two things can be true.\"\n\nFen pulled the line toward them and pointed to the section that would carry the actual load. Maui leaned closer, interested despite himself. He'd tied enough lines in his life to make the motions look effortless, but he was watching how Fen tested this particular wet rope.\n\n\"Is this how all of you pass the time?\" he asked. \"Inventing ways to make a demigod feel inadequate?\"\n\n\"Mostly we repair the damage caused by people who never check their knots.\"\n\nMaui looked wounded. Then he grinned and offered his uninjured hand.\n\n\"Fine. Tell me what the legendary Maui has to do to pass your tiny rope exam.\"\n\nHe listened to the answer. His hand was shaking by the end, partly from the cut and partly from how determined he was not to be the person who made the next boat unsafe. The grin stayed. So did the effort.\n\nFen slapped the rope away.\n\n\"Use the other one.\"\n\n\"You're very demanding for a person teaching knots.\"\n\n\"You're very loud for somebody who hasn't paid the repair bill.\"\n\nMaui blinked.\n\n\"There's a bill?\"\n\n\"There is always a bill.\"\n\nFen pointed to the ledger on the wall. Rope cost money. Nails cost money. Tamsin's time cost money. So did replacement planks. The Lanternweald's dock fees paid for some things, but not everything, and the people who kept boats floating weren't decorative figures in somebody else's epic.\n\nWe all looked at the ledger.\n\nTwo weeks of repairs had nearly emptied the landing fund.\n\n\"Why didn't you tell us?\" I asked.\n\n\"Because half the village offers to help for free and the other half feels guilty about not being able to,\" Fen said. \"I need the tools paid for. I don't need a parade.\"\n\nMaui was quiet.\n\nI wondered whether he was considering selling a legend to cover the price of nails.\n\nInstead he asked, \"How much for the rope?\"\n\nFen named an amount.\n\n\"I can pay some,\" Maui said.\n\n\"You don't owe the whole dock because you were here when it failed.\"\n\n\"I know. But I use it.\"\n\nFen looked surprised.\n\n\"That's different.\"\n\nI spent the afternoon helping inspect the mooring points. We marked each one with colored thread, not to rank them but to show which were safe, which needed maintenance, and which were closed until tested. Pip offered to translate the signs for travelers who couldn't read the Lanternweald script. Toph arrived and tapped the planks with her heel.\n\n\"Wood's talking,\" she announced.\n\n\"Does it have good news?\" I asked.\n\n\"Some of it's begging to be replaced.\"\n\nShe marked two supports we'd missed.\n\nMaui hated staying on the bench. He was especially offended when a small child offered him a cup of water and called him brave for sitting still.\n\n\"That was sarcastic,\" he said after the child left.\n\n\"That was generous.\"\n\n\"You're enjoying this.\"\n\n\"Immensely.\"\n\nBy sunset the dock was closed, the replacement list was posted, and nobody had suffered another injury.\n\nI found Maui alone at the end of the cove. He held a short length of spare rope in his good hand, turning it over as if it were a puzzle.\n\n\"Fen taught me something,\" he said.\n\n\"A knot?\"\n\n\"No. Well, yes. But something else.\" He pulled one loop through another. \"When I was younger, people always wanted proof I was strong. Then when I became strong, they wanted proof I could do anything. I got used to being the person nobody had to ask.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"Does it frighten you to be asked to stop?\"\n\nHe took a long time to answer.\n\n\"It frightens me that if I stop, you might discover you never needed me.\"\n\nThe words made me ache.\n\n\"Maui.\"\n\nHe looked at me with an openness so unlike his stage voice that I barely recognized it.\n\n\"I need you,\" I said. \"But not because you can hold a boat against the sea. I need the person who remembered I hate my tea too sweet. The one who painted a crab on a safety sign and then made sure it was the correct kind of crab.\"\n\n\"It is a very accurate crab.\"\n\n\"The most accurate.\"\n\nHe laughed once.\n\n\"I need you to tell me when you're scared,\" I continued. \"And I need you to let somebody else hold a rope when you're hurt.\"\n\nHe looked at his bandage.\n\n\"Even if they're bad at knots?\"\n\n\"Especially then. They can learn.\"\n\nHe looped his good arm around my shoulders. I leaned against him. His cheek brushed my hair, warm from the sun, and the smell of salt and timber rose between us.\n\n\"I could teach you the next knot,\" he murmured.\n\n\"Tomorrow. Tonight you're coming home.\"\n\n\"Bossy.\"\n\n\"You like it.\"\n\n\"Terribly.\"\n\nHe kissed me, slow enough that I could feel his smile become something gentler. We went home before the tide rose.\n\nThe following morning, the broken mooring was still broken. The ledger still needed funds. But Fen had drawn a new maintenance rota, Tamsin had priced the supplies, and Maui had signed up for a shift he could do with one hand.\n\nNobody owed him applause.\n\nHe seemed relieved.","word_count":1931},"maui-17":{"number":17,"title":"A Lantern for the Absent","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fourth expansion pass","content":"I couldn't decide what name to write on the lantern.\n\nThat was the trouble. There were names I remembered, names I had learned again, and names that lived just beyond my reach like objects at the bottom of dark water. If I wrote one, the others felt abandoned. If I wrote them all, I would have to admit how many empty spaces remained.\n\nMara watched me stare at the paper.\n\n\"You're allowed to leave it blank,\" she said.\n\n\"Everyone else is writing something.\"\n\n\"Everyone else is doing what they want.\"\n\nShe dipped her brush into a little pot of amber paint and continued a pattern of waves around her lantern. She had written nothing on it either.\n\n\"Who is yours for?\" I asked.\n\n\"Somebody who hated being named in public.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\n\"Then why bring it?\"\n\n\"Because I still want to carry a light.\"\n\nThat answer stayed with me.\n\nFrostturn wasn't quite winter in Lanternweald. The weather didn't obey any one homeworld's calendar. The name belonged to a local stretch of short days, when the first hard frost reached the canal reeds and the western wind brought the smell of cold seaweed into Lanternwell Square. The Lantern Walk at Frostturn had once been a way for travelers to find their doors before night overtook the paths. Nowadays the district stewards still checked the crossings, but people also carried lights for memory, gratitude, mourning, or no reason at all.\n\nPip had painted signs showing the accessible route. Edda and Tamsin had marked the stretches where the cobbles were uneven. Danse had tested the pier lamps, and Iroh had insisted on hot tea for anyone who needed to stop along the way.\n\nNobody had to dedicate a lantern to anyone.\n\nI knew this. I had helped write the notice.\n\nKnowing wasn't the same as believing it for myself.\n\nMaui swept toward the table carrying a basket of spare wicks and wearing a cloak with a fur collar so dramatic it could have belonged to a minor king. Someone had apparently told him the vigil wasn't a fashion event; he had treated that as a challenge. His grin eased when he saw my untouched lantern.\n\n\"I bring supplies,\" he announced, lifting the wicker basket like a trophy. \"For what I assume is an extremely sophisticated lantern-making ceremony.\"\n\n\"It's paper and string.\"\n\n\"And bravery. Don't undersell bravery.\"\n\nMini Maui had somehow acquired an imaginary fur collar of his own. He strutted across the larger tattooed chest until Maui pressed a finger against the tiny figure and told it to behave.\n\nI smiled despite the knot in my throat.\n\nHis expression softened a fraction. \"Hey. I don't know who you're writing to. I don't have to.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"But if you'd like company, my company is famously exceptional.\"\n\n\"And if I want to sit here without anyone talking?\"\n\nHe looked aghast. \"An unfair test of my greatest weakness.\"\n\nI laughed. He sat anyway, made a show of sealing his lips, and lasted perhaps forty seconds before whispering that he'd defeated a storage cabinet that morning. Then he caught himself, shrugged, and pushed the basket toward me. I didn't need him to become silent or saintly. I needed him to recognize that the unnamed people I mourned were real, even when he couldn't put himself in their story.\n\n\"How many enemies have you defeated?\" I asked.\n\n\"Today? None.\"\n\n\"Tragic.\"\n\n\"I did battle with a stubborn storage cabinet.\"\n\n\"Did you win?\"\n\n\"It surrendered two hinges and a spider.\"\n\nHe put the basket down and looked at my blank lantern.\n\n\"Too many names?\"\n\nI nodded.\n\nHe didn't ask which ones.\n\nHe sat beside me and threaded a wick through a little brass ring. His injured hand had healed enough for ordinary work, though Fen still wouldn't let him touch the heaviest mooring ropes.\n\nAfter a while he said, \"I used to tell stories where everyone remembered me.\"\n\n\"That's not a surprise.\"\n\n\"No. But I thought if I made the story big enough, nobody would forget the people I'd lost along the way. I could keep them with me by talking.\"\n\n\"Did it work?\"\n\n\"For a while. Then I realized some people wanted to be remembered quietly.\"\n\nI ran my thumb over the paper.\n\n\"Some of mine might not even know I'm here.\"\n\n\"Do you want to go looking for them?\"\n\n\"Someday. Maybe. Not because tonight tells me I have to.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI picked up the brush and painted a small circle in the center of the lantern. I left a gap at the top, an opening too small for a name but large enough for light.\n\n\"That's it?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"For now.\"\n\n\"It's beautiful.\"\n\n\"You haven't seen it lit.\"\n\n\"I know what I like.\"\n\nI smiled, then caught myself worrying that I had been unfair to him. He had listened to my grief so patiently, and I wasn't sure how to explain that loving him did not close the spaces I'd carried from before.\n\n\"Maui?\"\n\n\"Hm?\"\n\n\"Can I ask you something awkward?\"\n\n\"I thrive on awkward questions.\"\n\n\"Do you?\"\n\n\"Absolutely not. Ask anyway.\"\n\nI put down the brush.\n\n\"You know I'm ethically non-monogamous. I prefer polyamory. I've told you that from the beginning, and I haven't changed my mind.\"\n\nHis expression remained open.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I worry that when I miss people, you'll think I'm wishing you were someone else.\"\n\nHe looked at the lantern.\n\n\"I do wonder whether I'll be enough for the stories you haven't remembered.\"\n\n\"That's not a fair job to give you.\"\n\n\"I know.\" He hesitated. \"But being a hero makes it very tempting to think every problem needs me.\"\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"You don't need to replace anyone. You don't need to be the only person I love. I want you here because you're Maui, and because I choose you.\"\n\nHe let out a breath.\n\n\"And if you choose another person too?\"\n\n\"Then I tell the truth. I don't expect you to agree to something just because it makes sense to me. We talk about boundaries, and you get to decide what you want.\"\n\nHe was quiet for a moment.\n\n\"I want to be someone you can come home to,\" he said. \"Without making home a cage.\"\n\nI reached for his hand.\n\n\"That's what I want too.\"\n\nWe stayed at the table until the lanterns were ready. Then Mara called for help, and the square became a pleasant disaster of people trying to carry hot drinks, spare wicks, and weatherproof coats at the same time.\n\nIroh arrived with a kettle. Greg had brought a small hand drum so people could keep pace without turning the walk into a concert. Toph tested the route by feeling the ground beneath her boots, and Aang helped the children hang lanterns where they could reach them safely.\n\nMaui carried my lantern.\n\nHe didn't ask what it meant.\n\nThe procession began at Lanternwell. The bells rang once, soft and ordinary. Along the canal, the lights stretched into a line that seemed to float just above the water. Frost had caught in the reeds, making them shine at their edges.\n\nI walked beside Maui past Jasmine Steps, then toward the cove.\n\nA woman ahead of us stopped at a bench. She held a lantern painted with three small stars. Her face was wet. Nobody asked her to explain. Edda moved a cup of tea within reach, and the woman nodded her thanks.\n\nIt occurred to me that privacy could be a kind of hospitality. Not silence forced on someone who needed to speak, but room for the choice.\n\nAt the water, Maui lifted my lantern toward a hook beneath the signal post.\n\n\"Here?\"\n\n\"Here.\"\n\nHe hung it carefully.\n\nThe flame caught the circle I'd painted, spilling light through the little gap.\n\nFor one terrible, beautiful moment I wanted to tell him every name. I wanted to invent the names I couldn't remember, if only to fill the absence.\n\nInstead, I leaned against him and whispered, \"I miss them.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe didn't ask who.\n\nI turned my face into his shoulder. His hand settled at the back of my coat, warm and steady.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\" he asked quietly.\n\nI nodded.\n\nHis lips met mine beneath the lantern, gentle enough that grief could remain in the room without being crowded out. I kissed him again because I wanted to, not because it made the night less sad.\n\nLater we walked home after most of the lights had been extinguished. There were still several hanging along the safe route, each marked with a steward's name so no one would assume somebody else had put them out.\n\nAt our door, Maui paused.\n\n\"Do you want company?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And quiet?\"\n\n\"Mostly.\"\n\n\"I can do mostly.\"\n\nFen came down the shore steps carrying the lantern tally, wick scissors, and a bent metal bracket.\n\"The west hook comes out of service until Tamsin checks the beam,\" Fen said.\nMaui looked over. \"I could fix that.\"\n\"You could hold the ladder while I remove it.\"\nHis expression suggested somebody had refused to applaud the sunrise. Mini Maui acted out a terrible fall from a miniature ladder, then crossed his arms in a tiny performance of outrage.\n\"You're supposed to be on my side,\" Maui told his tattoo.\nFen handed him the ladder. He planted its feet in the sand and kept them steady while Fen loosened the bracket. He didn't offer to pull the beam apart, replace the whole pier, or demonstrate how strong he was. I knew how much effort that took.\n\"The lantern list doesn't require names,\" Fen told me. \"I only need to know which hooks are safe. Nobody checking the tally gets to decide whom you're missing.\"\nWhen Fen left, Maui flexed his hands and gave me an injured look.\n\"Not one monster. Not a single heroic rescue. I was magnificent at standing still, and there's no audience.\"\n\"I was watching.\"\n\"Ah. My favorite audience.\"\nThe humor slipped from his face when he saw the blank space on my lantern. He didn't ask me to fill it.\n\"Do you want me here?\" he asked.\nI took his hand. \"Yes. Not because you can hold a ladder.\"\n\"Good, because that ladder was vicious. Tried to stand upright the whole time.\"\nI kissed him, and for a moment he seemed surprised enough to forget his next boast. The lantern remained unmarked, the bracket stayed off the rail, and the tide went on moving without asking us to explain our grief.\n\nI laughed, tired and grateful. We sat with a blanket and a small bowl of warmed bread until the fire settled into coals.\n\nThe lantern at Tideglass burned until dawn.\n\nIt held no names.\n\nIt did not forget anyone.","word_count":1791},"maui-32":{"number":32,"title":"The Shape of an Agreement","subtitle":"Lanternweald — sixth expansion pass","content":"The calendar had survived salt spray, one dropped kettle, and three months of Maui writing appointments in letters large enough to be seen from the harbor. It did not survive his attempt to improve it.\n\nI found it on the kitchen table with a line drawn through Thursday.\n\nThursday had been ours.\n\nHe had replaced it with a note: *Helping Fen. Necessary.*\n\nI stood there holding a parcel from Moonroot Archive, still wearing the coat I'd put on that morning to come home early. We'd planned the evening two weeks ago. Not a festival, not a rescue, not a shift at the pantry. Dinner, the little blue blanket, and absolutely no public meetings.\n\n\"Maui?\"\n\nHe came in carrying a coil of rope. \"Before you say anything, the new mooring—\"\n\n\"You canceled our evening.\"\n\nHe looked at the calendar. \"Moved it! Brand-new day. It's like a heroic rescue, except for a dinner plan. Friday's open.\"\n\n\"Friday Iroh and I are reviewing the terrace map.\"\n\n\"Then Saturday.\"\n\n\"Saturday you promised Lio you'd inspect the skiff.\"\n\nHis smile faltered. \"Right.\"\n\nI set the parcel beside the stove. The air smelled like wet rope and rosemary. I wanted to say something spectacularly unfair, something about being last on his list. A familiar, uglier part of me wanted to win the argument before he knew it had begun.\n\nInstead I said, \"You didn't ask me.\"\n\nMaui put down the rope. \"Fen needed someone to help secure the landing before the wind shifts.\"\n\n\"Fen could need help. I might even agree that it's urgent. Neither changes the fact that you decided what happened to my evening without telling me.\"\n\n\"I thought you'd understand.\"\n\n\"I do understand the mooring. I don't understand why you didn't send a note.\"\n\nHe rubbed his face. \"I was busy.\"\n\n\"So busy you had time to rewrite the calendar.\"\n\nThat landed.\n\nHe looked at the enormous letters he'd written across Thursday. \"Okay. That was bad.\"\n\nI pulled out a chair. \"Sit with me?\"\n\nHe sat, awkwardly. His injured hand had healed, but he still curled his fingers when he was ashamed, as if bracing for a blow that wasn't coming.\n\n\"I made a mistake,\" he said. \"I should have asked.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"I need to know something, too.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"Sometimes I look at that calendar and feel like I'm asking for an appointment with someone who has six lives. Then I hate myself for thinking it, because you told me from the beginning that you're ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. I agreed to that knowing what it meant.\"\n\n\"I don't have six lives,\" I said. \"I have one. It's the same me coming to your door.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Knowing doesn't mean you stop feeling lonely.\"\n\nHe looked at me, surprised.\n\nI turned the calendar around. Its edges were curled. Under my appointments he'd added tiny drawings of fish and hooks. Beneath my private time with Severus, he'd written *No questions*. He had been trying, in his own untidy way, to respect agreements he didn't always find easy.\n\n\"Can we write down what we actually mean?\" I asked.\n\n\"Without making it a contract that gets me arrested?\"\n\n\"Preferably.\"\n\nWe took a clean sheet from the green notebook. Across the top I wrote *Our present agreements—not ownership, not forever.*\n\nMaui leaned over to read it.\n\n\"That's a long title.\"\n\n\"We can edit.\"\n\nWe agreed to tell each other about schedule changes before altering shared plans, unless there was an immediate emergency. In that case we would send word as soon as safely possible. We agreed not to share details about my other partners' private lives without their permission. He could ask for reassurance, and I could answer honestly without ranking anybody.\n\nMost importantly, either of us could say no to a date, a conversation, a kiss, or a request for time. No explanation was required to make a no count.\n\nMaui studied the last line.\n\n\"If you say no because you need quiet, can I ask when we might talk again?\"\n\n\"Yes. That's a request, not a demand. Sometimes I won't know.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"And if I need quiet?\"\n\n\"Then you get it too.\"\n\nWe tried the new agreement on a small problem before declaring it useful.\n\n\"Suppose Fen needs you during our next dinner,\" I said.\n\nMaui looked at the list. \"I ask whether it's an emergency.\"\n\n\"And if it is?\"\n\n\"I tell you where I'm going if there's time. I send word when I can. I don't decide what you'll do with the evening.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"And if it isn't?\"\n\n\"You can still choose to help, but you ask whether we can move our date before you strike it through.\"\n\nHe made a face. \"That means you might say no.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Terrible system.\"\n\nI poked him with the pencil. \"Try again.\"\n\nHe laughed. \"That means I need to decide what promise I'm keeping instead of assuming you'll wait.\"\n\nThe distinction mattered more than I expected. He could care about Fen, and he could care about me. Nobody had to become the villain because two good things asked for the same hour.\n\nI had told him I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred polyamory, but a relationship agreement wasn't just about who we kissed. It was about whether he could need reassurance without demanding my freedom, and whether I could choose to be with him without pretending all my other commitments had vanished.\n\n\"Would you like me to come with you to check the mooring tomorrow?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes. If you want to.\"\n\n\"I can do the morning. In the afternoon I'm at Moonroot.\"\n\nHe wrote both parts down. His handwriting was so large that the last word disappeared into the margin.\n\n\"There's no room,\" he complained.\n\n\"Then we'll need another page.\"\n\nHe looked at the green notebook and reached for a blank sheet. \"Are you sure we can change this later?\"\n\n\"We have to be able to. Otherwise it becomes another door we're afraid to open.\"\n\nHe nodded, and when I leaned close he waited for me to kiss him. The warmth of his hand at my waist felt like an invitation I was still free to accept tomorrow or refuse next week. That made it sweeter, not smaller.\n\nWe left space at the bottom to revise the page. We signed it with our names, not as a legal instrument but because the act made us both laugh. Maui drew a small fish beside his signature. Its expression was deeply suspicious.\n\nThe fish beside his name looked as though it were preparing to attack the calendar.\n\n\"That's you,\" I said.\n\nMaui drew himself up. \"That is a fearsome guardian of the Thursday dinner.\"\n\n\"It's a fish with a grudge.\"\n\n\"Exactly. Deadly.\"\n\nMini Maui poked his head out of the tattoos and began miming the fish's angry expression. The real Maui looked down and pointed a warning finger.\n\n\"No sabotage. We are handling this like adults.\"\n\n\"You sound disappointed.\"\n\n\"I am extremely disappointed. Handling things like adults is exhausting. Do you know how much easier it is to fight a monster?\"\n\nI laughed. He grinned with genuine relief, then tapped the crossed-out Thursday and the newly marked Sunday.\n\n\"Still want that dinner with me?\" he asked, quieter.\n\n\"Yes. On Sunday.\"\n\n\"Good. I'm going to make something so magnificent you'll write songs about it.\"\n\n\"I'll settle for edible.\"\n\nHis offended gasp echoed into the kitchen. He left for Fen's shift on time, but not before drawing a tiny crown on the fish.\n\nI glanced at the clock.\n\n\"You still have the mooring shift?\"\n\n\"Yes. Fen's expecting me.\"\n\n\"Then go. I don't want the landing to fail because we were arguing.\"\n\nHe paused at the door. \"Are we all right?\"\n\n\"We're working on it.\"\n\nHe seemed to take that more seriously than any grand assurance I could have given.\n\nThe next morning he sent a message before the agreed hour. Fen's inspection had uncovered a worn ring. They would need another half-day, and he wanted to know whether I minded moving our meal to Sunday.\n\nI smiled at the message.\n\n*Sunday works. Ask Lio whether Saturday still suits them.*\n\nHe answered with a picture of a fish whose fins were holding a calendar.\n\nOn Sunday, we made dinner together. Maui burned the first flatbread. I misread the spice jar and filled the kitchen with cinnamon. We ate the second batch outside by the sheltered garden, where the sea kale was growing slowly but stubbornly.\n\nA messenger stopped at the outer gate before we'd finished the meal. Maui's head turned at the sound of footsteps, and I saw the old reflex take hold: somebody from the landing, a problem to fix, an excuse to be needed before the dishes had cooled.\n\nThe messenger was Pip, carrying a small square of paper and wearing a look that could have curdled milk.\n\n“Before you make that face,” they told him, “nobody is drowning. Fen wanted you to know the evening shift changed. Tomorrow's still yours, with the new rope inspection.”\n\nMaui looked almost offended. “I wasn't making a face.”\n\n“Your tattoos were making it for you.”\n\nMini Maui had already drawn a heroic hook in the air and started waving it at imaginary waves. The tiny figure froze when it noticed us staring.\n\nPip handed him the note. “Fen said to read it, not run to the dock.”\n\nMaui read it. I watched his shoulders loosen as the words sank in.\n\n“I can bring the repair ledger in the morning,” he said.\n\n“Great. Then I'll go home.” Pip turned to me. “Sorry to interrupt your dinner.”\n\n“It's okay.”\n\n“No,” Maui said, surprising both of us. “It wasn't urgent. Next time leave the note in the message box. We'll read it after the meal.”\n\nPip gave him a long look, then grinned. “Look at that. He can be taught.”\n\nWhen they'd gone, Maui stared at the paper and laughed under his breath. “I'm going to be unbearable about how well I handled that.”\n\n“Going to be?”\n\nHe pulled the blue blanket over both our knees. His thigh settled against mine; his fingers, still rough from the rope, brushed my wrist. The touch was not an apology for Thursday. It was the sort of wanting that made me stop thinking about calendars for a moment.\n\n“There's a whole night left,” he said, voice lower than before.\n\n“Then don't spend it making a speech.”\n\nHis grin flashed. “I was about to make an excellent one.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nHe folded the note and put it facedown on the table.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\" he asked when we'd cleared the plates.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHis thumb brushed my cheek. He kissed me as though he didn't want the moment to finish, then drew back to ask whether I was cold.\n\n\"A little,\" I admitted.\n\nHe fetched the blue blanket.\n\nWe sat close, the sea breathing beyond the stones, and I let my head rest against him. Later we closed our door and kept the evening to ourselves.\n\nIn the morning the calendar was still hanging. Thursday was crossed out, Sunday carried a small blue star, and our new agreement lay beside the green notebook, ready to be revised when life made more honesty necessary.\n\nIt was paper.\n\nWe were the people who had to keep choosing what it meant.","word_count":1883},"maui-42":{"number":42,"title":"Where the Water Cannot Go","subtitle":"Lanternweald — sixth expansion pass","content":"Fen spread three maps on the table and weighted them down with stones.\n\nThe oldest showed a blue line running beneath the square toward Tideglass. The second, drawn after the Closed Bells, ended that line in thick black ink. The third was ours, covered in flood marks, inspection dates, and the small angry circles Tamsin drew wherever a repair still needed money.\n\n\"Water doesn't disappear because somebody closes a door,\" Fen said.\n\nToph tapped the table.\n\n\"Neither do the consequences.\"\n\nI stood beside Maui, watching Fen trace the old route.\n\nBefore the sealing, the passage had carried excess rainwater away from the terraces and floodplain into a controlled coastal outlet during certain tide conditions. It wasn't a miracle. It had gates, inspections, and periods when it couldn't be used safely. After the lower barrier was sealed, the valley had relied on smaller surface drains and a series of improvised diversions.\n\nThe diversions worked in ordinary weather.\n\nIn storms, they pushed water toward the same homes and gardens again and again.\n\nSela pointed to the map.\n\n\"Mine.\"\n\nThen another point.\n\nSela put two receipts beside the map: seedlings lost in the flood and repairs to the cart that carried them to market. Both had already been paid from her own savings.\n\"I don't need another lecture about how water finds a path,\" she said. \"I want to know why it keeps finding my garden.\"\nMaui leaned over the map, staring at the heaviest bank. \"I could move that before sunset.\"\n\"And flood the pottery kiln,\" Fen said. \"That's why we measure things before lifting them.\"\nToph put her heel against the floor. \"The ground tilts east. You shove the bank and somebody else gets your problem.\"\nMini Maui pressed both hands to his eyes. I touched his wrist, not to silence him, but to ask him to stay while other people finished speaking.\nTamsin marked three tests: an inspection of the upper surface-channel gate, a temporary footpath above the wet ground, and a fresh measurement at the next predicted high tide. The lower Closed Bells passage remained sealed. None of us had evidence that made reopening it safe.\nSela read the plan twice. \"And the market gets the results in writing. No festival advertisements before we know people can actually use the route.\"\n\"Agreed,\" I said.\nMaui picked up her receipts. \"Can I at least carry these to the funding desk?\"\n\"Only if they give you a signature,\" Sela said.\nMaui straightened to his full height. \"You're speaking to the greatest courier in the history of—\"\n\"Maui,\" I said.\n\"Right. Two receipts. Two signatures. Not even a tiny musical number.\"\nHe got both receipts signed. When he brought the copies back, Sela checked them before thanking him.\nIt was a small victory with an actual date on it. I liked seeing him proud of something nobody had asked him to perform.\n\n\"Mine too. I thought the floods were just getting worse.\"\n\n\"They may be,\" Fen said. \"But the blocked route has made the pattern less forgiving.\"\n\nNeri sat at the far end of the table with a stack of copied maintenance receipts. Their face was pale.\n\n\"The council kept repairing the surface channels,\" they said. \"We told ourselves we were improving the valley.\"\n\n\"You were repairing symptoms,\" Tamsin answered. \"Sometimes repairs were necessary. They weren't the whole plan.\"\n\nI felt a cold ache beneath my ribs.\n\nThe old decision had not ended when the keepers died or the records burned. It had become a pattern in the landscape, the kind people accepted because they had lived with it long enough.\n\nMaui stared at the map.\n\n\"There,\" he said, tapping the thick black line. \"Open it. Let the water go where it's trying to go.\"\n\nFen shot him a look. \"The last time somebody thought that, the passage closed on people.\"\n\n\"I know.\" His voice rose. \"That's why I want it open. Nobody should have to watch their house flood because someone a hundred years ago was afraid!\"\n\nMini Maui appeared on his chest, lifted a tiny imaginary gate, and immediately fell backward under the weight. Maui looked down and made an irritated sound.\n\n\"Great. Even my tattoos have joined the committee.\"\n\nToph put a hand on the table. \"You can lift a rock, big guy. You can't tell me whether the soil under it will hold.\"\n\nMaui's mouth opened. For a moment I expected another argument about his legendary strength. Then he stared at the garden marked beyond the line and shut it again.\n\n\"Fine. Tell me what you *can* check,\" he said to Fen. \"And then give me something heavy to do that won't make it worse.\"\n\nThe demand was still loud. The choice to listen was real. They could exist in the same sentence without erasing each other.\n\n\"Then open the barrier.\"\n\nFen shook their head.\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\n\"People are getting wet.\"\n\n\"And they'd get much wetter if an unstable tunnel failed under the spring.\"\n\nThe silence was uncomfortable.\n\nDanse laid out the inspection results from the upper chamber. The mortar was different in two sections. One support showed long-term movement. We could test the surface outlet and keep the temporary channels open, but the lower route required engineering work before any attempt at full reopening.\n\nI wanted to argue.\n\nInstead I asked what the current closure actually meant.\n\n\"The upper maintenance hatch can be opened under supervision for inspection,\" Tamsin said. \"The lower flood barrier remains closed. Surface drains need additional capacity before the next storm.\"\n\nEdda raised her hand.\n\n\"And the detour must be usable by people who can't take the steps to the upper ridge.\"\n\nWe rewrote the work list.\n\nThe council would fund an accessible temporary path and a monitored surface overflow. Tideglass would provide tide data, Riverstone would oversee drainage effects on gardens, Brasswatch would test the portable pump and new fittings, and Moonroot would preserve the old plans without claiming they gave anyone permission to enter unsafe spaces.\n\nThe work needed money and patience.\n\nNeither was popular.\n\nAfter the meeting, I found Neri by the fountain.\n\nThey were staring at the old stone arch.\n\n\"I used to think the sealed route was part of our history,\" they said. \"Something unfortunate and finished.\"\n\n\"It wasn't finished.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey looked at me.\n\n\"Do you hate me for not asking sooner?\"\n\nI had to think before answering.\n\n\"I'm angry that people kept suffering. I'm glad you're asking now. Both are true.\"\n\nThey nodded slowly.\n\n\"That is more than I expected.\"\n\nThe temporary channel was built over the following week. It ran along the safe upper path, using removable sections that could be cleaned without entering the old tunnel. Sela helped place the runoff markers near the garden. Edda tested the detour in wet weather. Toph checked the supports while Tamsin logged the movement.\n\nMaui carried materials when asked and learned the names of three workers who had previously been invisible to him. He brought hot food from the pantry after the late shift, then stayed to wash the pots.\n\nHalfway through installing the last removable section, the tide turned sooner than Fen had predicted. Water pressed against the temporary barrier and the nearest joint began to shiver.\n\nMaui planted his feet and caught the side beam before it could shift.\n\n“I've got it!” he shouted.\n\nToph felt the movement under her boots. “You have the beam. You don't have the foundation, Muscles.”\n\nEdda had already called for the people on the lower walk to move behind the marked line. Fen checked the tide gauge and told the crew to stop feeding more water through the test section.\n\n“Let me lift it,” Maui offered. “I can move the whole thing onto higher ground.”\n\nTamsin looked at the bolts he was holding. “If you lift now, you'll tear out the anchors on the other end.”\n\nFor an instant he seemed ready to argue. His hook flashed in his hand. I knew the kind of story he liked: one enormous effort, a magnificent rescue, cheers and a place beside the fire where everyone would repeat what he'd done.\n\nThen he lowered the hook.\n\n“Tell me what to hold,” he said.\n\nEdda directed him to support one beam while the workers released pressure from the inspection gate. Toph checked the ground, Tamsin resecured the movable brackets and Fen recorded the tide height at which the test had failed. I held the tools, dry enough to use, and tried not to think about how close the water had come to soaking the market stalls.\n\nWhen the barrier stopped moving, nobody cheered. The crew had to revise the safe operating range and postpone opening the upper route during high tide. A closed gate was frustrating. It was better than a false promise.\n\nMaui wiped saltwater from his forehead. “Well,” he said, “I only saved one beam.”\n\n“You listened to three people while saving it,” Toph said. “That's your new record.”\n\nHe looked offended for a moment. Then he grinned. “Someone write that down. I want it on a monument.”\n\nTamsin handed him the inspection sheet. “Sign here instead.”\n\nHe did, beneath the workers whose measurements had actually kept the channel standing.\n\nThe work did not make him smaller.\n\nI was beginning to think it made him more himself.\n\nOn the final test day, a controlled flow of water ran through the temporary channel and out toward the coast. Fen watched the gauges. Danse checked the pump. Sela stood beside the garden, waiting to see whether her soil would flood again.\n\nThe first test passed.\n\nThe second revealed a small leak near the lower joint.\n\nWe stopped and repaired it.\n\nThe third passed within the limits the team had written down.\n\nNobody cheered until Fen said the words.\n\n\"Safe for the posted conditions.\"\n\nThen we cheered.\n\nSela insisted on walking the team through the garden once the test ended. The new flow had missed the lowest bed, but a narrow strip of soil beside the path was damp in a way it hadn't been before.\n\n\"I want that measured tomorrow,\" she said. \"It may be nothing. My seedlings don't care whether we call it nothing.\"\n\nFen agreed and added the observation to the test sheet. Tamsin marked the difference between water that had been deliberately released during the controlled trial and rain they couldn't predict. Edda asked whether the temporary channel would still leave an accessible route if one section had to be removed for cleaning.\n\n\"We can test that,\" Danse answered.\n\n\"Before the next storm,\" she said.\n\nToph rested her feet against the path and made a face. \"At least the stone isn't shifting.\"\n\n\"Not shifting under the conditions we measured,\" Tamsin corrected.\n\nToph laughed. \"You engineers are contagious.\"\n\nMaui watched the exchange without offering to move the entire channel. I was absurdly proud of him.\n\nWhen the work stopped for a break, he sat beside me on a low wall.\n\n\"I was THIS close to announcing that I saved the entire valley,\" he admitted, spreading his arms. \"Then Sela mentioned her seedlings and Fen mentioned the gauges. Nobody understands the hard life of a humble legend.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Is that wrong?\"\n\n\"No. It's a thing we want, not a fact we can claim yet.\"\n\nHe looked out toward Tideglass. \"You make hope sound like paperwork.\"\n\n\"Sometimes hope needs a place to put the numbers.\"\n\nHe smiled at that, and when I leaned against him his arm settled around me without pulling me away from the garden. We could care about the place and each other at the same time.\n\nI thought of the old gate beneath the Closed Bells line. It remained sealed for reasons we were still trying to understand. We didn't have to open it to prove the work we'd done mattered. For now, the new surface route had passed a limited test, and people who lived beside it had the right to question what came next.\n\nThe cheering stopped as soon as Fen asked us to record the test conditions.\n\n\"If the tide rises past the marked line, close the channel. If the debris screen blocks, close it until it's cleared. If nobody on duty answers, don't improvise.\"\n\nMaui rested on his shovel. \"You have an extraordinary talent for ruining celebrations.\"\n\n\"And keeping people alive for the next one,\" Fen answered.\n\nSela asked about frozen winter ground. Fen admitted the team had no winter data. Rather than declare permanent success, they wrote down what needed monitoring. Edda tested the route in the evening light and pointed out a marker that disappeared against the wet paving. Tamsin moved it, then asked her to try again.\n\nNeri posted three notices: the approved operating conditions, the name of each duty worker, and a private route for residents to report flooding. Nobody would have to post a home address or a medical need beside the bakery menu. Two people outside the repair crew would review disputes.\n\n\"That seems rather formal,\" Maui said.\n\n\"Water can be extremely formal when it reaches somebody's kitchen,\" Sela replied.\n\nEven Danse smiled.\n\nA child asked whether the new channel would stay forever. Fen crouched beside the diagram.\n\n\"We don't know. We test things, fix them, and sometimes replace them.\"\n\n\"So something can work without being finished?\"\n\n\"Especially that,\" Toph said.\n\nWe put the tools away before rain arrived. The unknown signature in Moonroot's Closed Bells records was still unknown, and no newly approved surface test gave us permission to cross the old underground barrier. But for tonight Sela could go home with a dry garden and Edda could use the path. That felt worth celebrating properly.\n\nThe lower passage remained closed.\n\nThat evening, Maui found me sitting on the bench above Tideglass. I was tired enough that the sea seemed to move in slow pieces.\n\n\"Disappointed?\" he asked.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\n\"Because we didn't open the old route?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe sat beside me.\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nI leaned against him.\n\n\"But the gardens are safer,\" I said.\n\n\"And people can use the path.\"\n\n\"And the inspection records are public.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Not exactly a great legend.\"\n\n\"Perhaps we should stop measuring our lives by whether they make good legends.\"\n\nHe pretended to consider.\n\n\"That sounds like more work.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nHe kissed me softly.\n\nBelow us, the water moved through the new surface channel, doing what water had always tried to do.\n\nFinding somewhere safe to go.","word_count":2398},"maui-48":{"number":48,"title":"The Lantern Ledger","subtitle":"Lanternweald — sixth expansion pass","content":"The new ledger had no columns for loyalty.\n\nThat was Neri's first rule.\n\nWe sat at a long table in Moonroot Archive, comparing the surviving pages of the old arrival book with a clean blank volume. The old book had been designed for people who believed that crossing into Lanternweald created a debt. It recorded names, expected return dates, assigned keepers, and—in several places—marks that might have indicated whether someone had been permitted to leave.\n\nSome of those marks made my skin crawl.\n\n\"Are we keeping any of this?\" I asked.\n\nNeri touched the cover.\n\n\"We need to preserve the original as evidence. We do not need to make a new version of its authority.\"\n\nSeverus, working at the next desk, looked up.\n\n\"An excellent distinction.\"\n\nOrin had prepared a proposed format for the public record. It listed structural events, maintenance decisions, funding approvals, and testimony categories. Personal travel details would be kept only when someone requested help and only for as long as the service required. Romantic relationships were not part of the civic record.\n\nThe reading table had been lowered after Edda watched three visitors struggle to reach its writing surface. One leg was still propped on a wedge of scrap wood. Whenever Maui leaned forward, the ink bottle rattled.\n\n\"Stop attacking the furniture,\" Severus said.\n\n\"I'm reading,\" Maui replied.\n\n\"Then your technique requires revision.\"\n\nMaui stepped away with theatrical dignity. \"Observe. Flawless reading posture.\"\n\nOrin pressed the table's corner. It shifted anyway.\n\n\"The repair order is still open,\" they said. \"The record isn't public in any useful sense if half the people who need it can't write on the table.\"\n\nA charcoal line on the floor marked a turning space. Someone had moved the chair across it.\n\nI moved the chair back and fetched the order. One line specified the table leg, another a lamp that shone directly into a reader's eyes. Neither would reveal the erased keeper's name, but both mattered to the person who wanted to read the record.\n\nMaui raised his hook. \"I can hold the table steady all day.\"\n\n\"An admirable proposal,\" Severus said, \"if the archive intends to employ you as permanent furniture.\"\n\n\"Very handsome furniture.\"\n\n\"We have a maintenance budget,\" Orin said. \"We will use it.\"\n\nI noted the temporary assistance arrangement until the repair could be tested. Neri approved the sign. Severus returned to his evidence list. Maui put down his hook, apparently devastated that a perfectly good heroic job had been assigned to a carpenter.\n\n\"Nobody appreciates an architectural legend,\" he muttered.\n\n\"Nobody wants to write on one,\" I said.\n\nI wanted to hug the person who'd written that sentence.\n\nInstead I told Orin it was good work.\n\nThey looked delighted.\n\nThe hardest part was the blank line.\n\nIt belonged to the erased signature from the Closed Bells ledger. The investigation had documented the order, the materials, the closure, and Neri's witness account of a person denied departure. But the name on the original page was gone, and none of the surviving evidence established it with sufficient confidence.\n\nAvel, the councilor who had demanded a name before, came to review the draft.\n\n\"You're leaving the blank?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" Neri said.\n\n\"People will think you're protecting someone.\"\n\n\"Then the note beside it should explain exactly why it's blank,\" Severus answered.\n\nI turned the page around so Avel could read it.\n\n*Signature removed from the surviving document. Identity unverified. The harm caused by the closure is documented independently. Additional evidence may be submitted without mandatory public disclosure.*\n\nAvel read it twice.\n\nHe looked tired.\n\n\"I want someone to answer for what happened.\"\n\n\"So do I,\" I said. \"That doesn't give us permission to choose a person because the empty space makes us angry.\"\n\nHe sat down.\n\nFor a moment I thought he might begin another argument. Instead he asked to see the evidence list.\n\nWe showed him the invoices, tide charts, witness summary, and the notes explaining what each could and could not prove. We also showed him the emergency policy changes already enacted because of the investigation: voluntary departure routes, clear access to the upper hall, no public lists of destinations, and reviewable decisions during closures.\n\nHe studied them.\n\n\"This is more than a blank,\" he said finally.\n\n\"Yes,\" Neri answered. \"It is the work around it.\"\n\nBefore we released the draft, Orin caught a problem in the access rules.\n\nThe next morning a traveler came to the archive with a small folded ticket and a question about the entry for their family. They didn't want their destination made public. They wanted to know whether the date on the record could be corrected without showing everyone where they'd gone.\n\nNeri invited them to sit at the lower reading table. The table had been repaired the previous week, but the lamp above it still needed a shade. Orin moved the lamp rather than asking the traveler to change seats.\n\n“May I see the original ticket?” Neri asked.\n\nThe traveler hesitated, then passed it over. The name on the card matched the restricted register; the time did not. The paper was stained with salt, and I could see why a tired clerk might have read the figures incorrectly.\n\n“Will the correction be visible?” the traveler asked.\n\n“The fact that a correction occurred, yes,” Neri answered. “Not your private route. The public ledger will show a dated amendment to the timetable, and the restricted file will keep the ticket with the reason.”\n\nThey thought about that. “Who gets to see the restricted version?”\n\n“Only the archive staff authorized to handle it, unless you give specific permission otherwise.”\n\nMaui stood behind me with the parcel of blank forms he'd carried from the printer. He was unusually quiet. Mini Maui had started drawing a little heroic stamp on his forearm, but the real Maui covered it with two fingers.\n\n“Good,” the traveler said. “Then you can write it.”\n\nOrin entered the correction while they watched. The old date stayed legible, crossed with a single line and signed beside the new one. No magic erased the original mistake, and no one acted as though admitting it had ruined the archive.\n\nAfter they left, Maui set down his parcel.\n\n“Do you think they're happy?” he asked.\n\n“I don't know. They got what they asked for.”\n\n“That is a very small victory.”\n\n“Small for you.”\n\nHe looked at the corrected date, then at the empty space where the private destination had not been copied. “Right,” he said. And he went to fetch another stack of forms without trying to make himself the hero of the story.\n\n\"A public correction,\" they said, tracing the margin, \"might reproduce somebody's private statement if we're not careful. Suppose a person gives us an account in confidence and later asks us to correct the date. Do we announce the whole account to prove we fixed it?\"\n\nNeri looked at the page.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then the correction procedure needs two paths,\" Orin said. \"One for public evidence and one for confidential testimony.\"\n\nSeverus put down his pen.\n\n\"That is not a minor adjustment.\"\n\nOrin's face fell.\n\n\"It's a necessary one,\" he continued. \"My objection is to how easily we could have overlooked it.\"\n\nWe drew two parallel columns. Public documents could receive a dated correction note that cited the original. Confidential statements would be reviewed with their contributor, with only an appropriately limited public summary when the contributor agreed. Emergency information could be used for safety without turning a frightened traveler's identity into festival gossip.\n\nA visitor from Tideglass arrived with a damaged invoice. They wanted to know whether the bill had been paid before the old closure, but they did not want their family's name displayed.\n\n\"Can you check without publishing it?\" they asked.\n\nNeri explained the process we'd just written.\n\n\"Good,\" the visitor said. \"Then I'd like you to look.\"\n\nThe transaction was small, one box of hinges. It did not name the missing keeper or resolve the Closed Bells. Orin recorded what it did establish and carefully marked the rest unknown.\n\nI felt a strange relief. The blank signature wasn't the only place where uncertainty mattered. Every small record needed a way for people to challenge it without surrendering the privacy the new rules were meant to protect.\n\nWhen the visitor left, Severus looked at Orin.\n\n\"You have improved the procedure.\"\n\nOrin smiled.\n\n\"By finding a mistake?\"\n\n\"That is generally how improvement begins,\" he said.\n\nMaui, carrying the empty paper wrapping from the printer, leaned in.\n\n\"You need an expert in mistakes? Lucky day. I've made some legendary ones. None came with such spectacularly boring handwriting, but I can adapt.\"\n\n\"Please refrain,\" Severus answered.\n\nI laughed, and Neri added the correction policy to the first page where every reader could see it.\n\nThe ledger was opened to public review the following week.\n\nPeople came with questions, corrections, and memories. Some brought documents; others asked whether they could give testimony privately. One person declined to leave a name and was told that was their choice. Another requested a copy of the revised emergency rules for a homeworld council dealing with flood evacuations.\n\nNeri gave them the public version.\n\nNobody received keys to private files merely because they were curious.\n\nMaui came in during the second afternoon, carrying a parcel of paper from the printer. He set it on the table and looked at the ledger.\n\n\"A ledger without a column for the biggest hero in the room?\" he asked, setting down the parcel. \"I demand a recount.\"\n\n\"There isn't a contest,\" Neri said.\n\n\"Then why am I losing?\"\n\nMini Maui drew an enormous star on his chest, struck it out, and pointed to the unfilled private record. For a moment Maui's expression turned thoughtful.\n\n\"People used to write down who belonged to whom?\" he asked.\n\n\"Who had authority to send travelers away or keep them here,\" Neri said.\n\nHis jaw tightened. He could joke about a competition, but the idea of somebody being trapped by a mark in a book seemed to anger him in a way the missing line never had.\n\n\"All right,\" he said, a little too loudly. \"New rule. If somebody wants to go, you let them go. If they want to come back, you ask whether they want company. If they want bread—\"\n\n\"Mara handles bread,\" I interrupted.\n\n\"She does. Very well. The great Maui merely delivers it.\"\n\nHe grinned again, but he didn't try to fill the blank line. It wasn't his story to complete.\n\n\"That's the famous blank line?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" I said.\n\nHe leaned closer.\n\n\"Looks smaller than I imagined.\"\n\n\"Most important things do.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\nSeverus made a disapproving noise.\n\n\"Please do not encourage him.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nLater, when the visitors had gone, I found Neri standing alone by the display case.\n\n\"How are you?\" I asked.\n\nThey considered.\n\n\"Less certain than I once was. More responsible, perhaps.\"\n\n\"That sounds exhausting.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nThey smiled faintly.\n\n\"I thought telling the truth would finally let me stop carrying it.\"\n\n\"Did it?\"\n\n\"No. But now other people can help carry the work.\"\n\nI looked at the new ledger.\n\nThe old book would remain in the archive, preserved with its flaws. The new one would be revised when evidence changed or policies failed. Its pages were numbered, its handling rules public, and its blank lines explained instead of quietly filled by whoever held the pen.\n\nMaui waited for me at the arch.\n\n\"Ready to go home?\" he asked.\n\n\"In a minute.\"\n\nI wrote one final note in the green notebook.\n\n*Accountability is not a perfect story. It is a record that lets other people ask questions tomorrow.*\n\nI closed it.\n\nThen I took Maui's hand and went through the open passage toward the square, where the lanterns were coming on one by one.\n\nThe next day a young visitor came back with a correction.\n\nTheir family's crossing had been entered as a departure at dusk, but the ferry hadn't sailed until the following morning. The public map used that time to estimate the last safe crossing, and the error made the old route look more reliable than it was.\n\nOrin found the source note.\n\n\"You're right,\" they said. \"The time was copied from the departure request, not the actual tide record.\"\n\nNeri asked whether the visitor wanted to attach a private statement or have the correction attributed anonymously.\n\n\"Anonymous, please.\"\n\n\"Then that's what we'll do.\"\n\nThe public page received a dated correction and a link to the weather record. Nobody wrote the visitor's name beside it. The next ferry schedule carried the revised time.\n\nI watched the process with a feeling I couldn't quite name. We had spent so much time worrying about a missing historical signature that I'd forgotten how much a smaller error could matter to somebody trying to get home.\n\nSeverus came to inspect the update. He checked the reference, nodded once, and said, \"A better ledger is one that can be corrected without a trial.\"\n\nOrin grinned.\n\nMaui leaned over my shoulder.\n\n\"And one that doesn't expect me to understand every column?\"\n\n\"That may be asking too much,\" I said.\n\nHe laughed.\n\nThere were ordinary lives on both sides of every line we kept. That was what the new book had to remember.\n\nThe ledger stayed where it belonged.\n\nNot beneath a crown.\n\nOn a table that could be reached.","word_count":2226}};var s=lib.series.find(x=>x.series_slug==="the-shore-i-choose");if(!s)return;for(var i=0;i<s.chapters.length;i++){var c=s.chapters[i],e=edits[c.id];if(e)Object.assign(c,e);}s.total_word_count=88429;})();
